 "events": [
  {
   "year": 1790,
   "kind": "census",
   "title": "The first federal census",
   "text": "1,733 Baltimore households, 281 free Black residents and 1,043 enslaved people. These are the least reliable figures on this page. The household rows account for only 79 per cent of the population the census itself recorded for the city, so treat every 1790 number as a floor with materially lower confidence than the rest."
  },
  {
   "year": 1800,
   "kind": "census",
   "title": "Free and enslaved almost level",
   "text": "2,614 free Black Baltimoreans against 2,752 enslaved. In ten years the free population had gone from a few hundred to nearly the size of the enslaved population. Slaveholding was at its widest reach in the city this year, in 26.0 per cent of all households, and it never reached that far again."
  },
  {
   "year": 1810,
   "kind": "census",
   "title": "Slavery peaks and stops growing",
   "text": "4,649 enslaved people, the largest number Baltimore ever recorded. Free Black residents pass them this year at 5,658. From here the two lines move apart in opposite directions for fifty years."
  },
  {
   "year": 1819,
   "kind": "directory",
   "title": "The earliest year we can map",
   "text": "Jackson's Baltimore Directory lists Black householders with addresses. 526 residents parsed from the volume, 92 placed on a map. The addresses mostly say near something rather than giving a corner, which is why so few of them resolve. This year exists on borrowed labour, transcribed from the page by Louis S. Diggs, Sr."
  },
  {
   "year": 1820,
   "kind": "census",
   "title": "The peak: 23.4 per cent of the city",
   "text": "10,326 free Black residents and 4,357 enslaved, together 23.4 per cent of Baltimore. This is the highest Black share the city ever records in this period. It is also the first year with a printed ward table, which used twelve wards rather than the twenty in use by 1850."
  },
  {
   "year": 1822,
